                  <provenance type="#e m"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_693">London. Hospital of
                        St Bartholomew, Smithfield</orgName><p>Given to the library by John
                        Wakering, master of the hospital 1423--66. Contains notes in the hand of
                        John Cok (d. c. 1468), brother and renter of the house.</p><p>'Memorandum
                        quod frater Iohannes Whyt quondam magister hospitalis sancti Bartholomei
                        Londinensis qui obit xv die Ianuarii anno domini Mo CCCCmo xxiijo qui dedit
                        hanc bibliam mediante magistro Iohanne Wakeryng successorem suum ad opus
                        ipsius hospitalis ibidem in perpetuum permansurum.'</p><p>On fols.
                        489r--499v Brother John Cok added summaries of the Bible and (fols.
                        498r--499v) an exposition of Jerome's general prologue, in 1463 and 1464
                        when he was aged 68 and 69.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">John Stow commented that 'since the spoyle of
                     that Library I haue seen this booke in the custody of my worshipfull friend, M.
                     Walter Cope' (Stow, Survey of London, 2. 23). 'The fayrest Bible that I have
                     seen.'</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">'Ex libris der Herzogin Elisabeth Sophie
                     Marie von Braunschweig'; belonged to her in 1752.</provenance>
               </history>
